# {{.SourceRepo}}

The root organization of the `{{.GithubOrganization}}` GitHub organization, kept
by bedrock. `{{.Prefix}}` is its prefix, and this repository is its record:
`placement.json` at the repository root says what the organization is, and the
workflow in `{{.WorkflowFile}}` keeps it true, by GitHub's API, on every pull
request and every merge.

This root organization has no cloud. Nothing of Google's exists for it: no
project, no bucket, no Cloud Build. It holds libraries alone, repositories that
deploy nowhere, and everything it keeps is on GitHub. There is no terraform here
either, and no state file: each run reads what stands on GitHub and compares it
with the placement.

The running log is [JOURNAL.md](JOURNAL.md). A step taken by hand gets a line
there first.

## What this organization keeps

For the GitHub organization:

- this repository: its merge settings (squash alone) and the ruleset on
  `{{.GithubDefaultBranch}}`, which takes changes by pull request alone, with
  {{.ApprovingReviewCount}} approving review(s), once the check
  `{{.PlanJobName}}` has passed;
- the environment `{{.RootEnvironment}}` of this repository, which admits the
  branch `{{.GithubDefaultBranch}}` alone and holds the infrastructure app's
  key ("The GitHub Apps and their keys", below);
- the GitHub Apps it acts as, held to what bedrock declares for each.

For each library the placement lists ("Libraries", below):

- the repository, made when it is missing, public or private as its entry
  says;
- its merge settings, the same as this repository's;
- the ruleset `default branch` on its default branch: pull requests alone, the
  same reviews, and the checks its entry names;
- the release app's credentials, where its release workflow reads them.

And once for all the libraries: the organization's ruleset `version tags`, under
which the release app alone creates, moves or deletes a tag named `v*` or
`*/v*`.

bedrock writes no file in a library. A library's workflows are its own, written
by the people who keep it.

## Bootstrapped by one command

The organization was made by `bedrock org bootstrap`, run once at a terminal by
an owner of the GitHub organization. It needs no Google credentials and no
token: what it does on GitHub it does as the infrastructure app it makes. In
order:

1. It makes the GitHub Apps in your browser, from the manifests bedrock
   declares: Create, then Install for all repositories, for each. The
   infrastructure app always; the release app when the placement lists a
   library.
2. It makes this repository, private, with an initial commit.
3. It makes the organization as the placement says, through the same engine the
   workflow runs: this repository's settings and ruleset, the environment with
   the infrastructure app's key sealed into it, each library, the version tags'
   ruleset, and the release app's credentials sealed where the libraries read
   them. Each key goes from GitHub's answer into its secret and is never written
   to this computer's disk.
4. It pushes `placement.json`, this README and the workflow as the first commit.

A run that stopped finishes on the next run with the same directory, with one
thing to know: GitHub never gives a sealed key back, and a run holds a key only
while it is the run that made the app. A later run acts with a key you hand it.
On the infrastructure app's settings page (the organization's settings, GitHub
Apps), generate a private key, then, in a terminal:

```
bedrock org bootstrap <dir> --infrastructure-app <its App ID> --infrastructure-key <the downloaded file>
```

and delete the file.

## How the root organization is kept

Every change goes by pull request: edit `placement.json`, run
`bedrock org render`, commit both. The workflow has three jobs.

- **`{{.PlanJobName}}`**, on a pull request into `{{.GithubDefaultBranch}}`:
  it compares the owned files with what the placement renders
  (`bedrock org check`), says what the merge would change on GitHub
  (`bedrock org plan`) and posts that on the pull request. The ruleset requires
  it.
- **`{{.ApplyJobName}}`**, on a merge: it makes the organization as the merged
  placement says (`bedrock org apply`). One apply runs at a time.
- **`{{.CheckJobName}}`**, once a week and when started by hand (the Actions
  tab, Run workflow): it compares the organization with the placement on its
  own clock and keeps one issue in this repository with what it found, opened
  when there is a finding and closed when there is none.

Each job acts as the infrastructure app, with a token it mints from the app's
key. Three things keep that key from a pull request:

- The plan runs on `pull_request_target`, so its steps are the ones on
  `{{.GithubDefaultBranch}}`. The pull request's files are read as data and
  never run.
- The bedrock that runs is the one `placement.json` pins (`bedrockVersion`): a
  release, checked against the checksum pinned beside it (`bedrockSha256`), or
  a commit on a branch of bedrock's repository, built where Go's checksum
  database verifies it.
- The key is a secret of the environment `{{.RootEnvironment}}`, which admits
  the branch `{{.GithubDefaultBranch}}` alone. A workflow on any other branch,
  whoever pushed it, is never handed the key.

The workflow file is bedrock's: `bedrock org render` writes it and
`bedrock org check` compares it, so a change made to it by hand fails the plan.
`bedrock upgrade` moves the pin.

## Libraries

A library is one entry under `libraries` in `placement.json`:

```json
"libraries": [
  {
    "repository": "example-library",
    "public": true,
    "checks": [
      "tests",
      "lint / Run the linters"
    ]
  }
]
```

- **`repository`** is the repository's name in `{{.GithubOrganization}}`.
- **`public`** makes it public; left out, it is private. The apply brings the
  repository to it either way, so read a pull request that sets it with care:
  a repository made public shows its whole history.
- **`checks`** are the checks a pull request into its default branch must pass,
  each by the name a pull request shows it under: a job's name, or, for a job
  of a reusable workflow, the calling job's id, a slash and the called job's
  name (`lint / Run the linters`, above, is the job `Run the linters` of the
  reusable workflow the job `lint` calls). GitHub Actions is the reporter of
  each.
- **`adopted`** (`true`) says the repository stood before the placement listed
  it, one transferred into the organization. The apply brings its settings and
  its ruleset to the entry like any library's. While the entry names no checks,
  the plan prints the checks the ruleset it came with requires: copy them into
  `checks`, then delete that ruleset on GitHub.

Since bedrock writes no workflow, a name in `checks` is only as good as the
repository's workflow files. Every plan and every weekly check reads the checks
the latest pull request into each library reported and says two things: a
required check that pull request did not report, which is a finding, since
every pull request will wait for it; and the checks it reported that the entry
does not require, which is a note, since only a person can tell a helper job
from a test nobody listed.

A library taken off the list is not deleted. The plan says so, the apply takes
the release app's credentials from it and its version tags out of the rule,
and the repository stays as it is until a person archives it on GitHub.

## The larger runner

`ciLargeRunner` in `placement.json` names the runner the libraries' workflows run their
long jobs on. The apply sets it as the organization variable `CI_LARGE_RUNNER`, which a
workflow reads where it chooses its runner
(`runs-on: ${{"{{"}} vars.CI_LARGE_RUNNER || 'ubuntu-latest' {{"}}"}}`). With `ciLargeRunnerSize`
(`8-core`, say) the apply creates that runner itself: a GitHub-hosted runner of the name
and size in the runner group `applications`, with at most `ciLargeRunnerMaximum` running
at once. The group admits public repositories when the placement lists a public library;
a group that did not would leave a public library's jobs waiting.

GitHub bills a larger runner per minute, for a public repository too, and a pull request
from a fork runs on it once its workflows are approved. The variable is bedrock's: one set
by hand is removed by the next apply while the placement names no runner.

## Releases

A library's release workflow acts as the release app, the one actor the
`version tags` ruleset lets make a release's tag. bedrock keeps what that
workflow reads, for the library repositories alone:

- the organization secret `{{.ReleaseClientIDOrgSecret}}`, the app's client
  ID;
- the organization secret `{{.ReleaseKeyOrgSecret}}`, its private key;
- the organization variable `{{.ReleaseLoginVariable}}`, its login, the author
  a release workflow accepts a release from.

A repository that is not a library reads neither secret.

## The GitHub Apps and their keys

| app | what it does | where its key is |
|---|---|---|
| `{{.AppName "infrastructure"}}` | creates and configures the organization's repositories, as this repository's workflow | the secret `{{.EnvironmentKeySecret}}` of this repository's environment `{{.RootEnvironment}}` |
| `{{.AppName "release"}}` | cuts the libraries' releases; made when the placement lists a library | the organization secret `{{.ReleaseKeyOrgSecret}}`, read by the libraries alone |

`placement.json` records each app's App ID. `bedrock org check` holds each app
to what bedrock declares: owned by `{{.GithubOrganization}}`, the declared
permissions and no other, installed for all repositories.

GitHub keeps a secret's value and never gives it back, so a key is rotated by
making a new one. On the app's settings page (the organization's settings,
GitHub Apps), generate a private key; then, in a terminal in a clone of this
repository:

```
bedrock secret add {{.InfrastructureKeyName}} --from-file <the downloaded file>
bedrock secret add {{.ReleaseKeyName}} --from-file <the downloaded file>
```

whichever app the key is of. The command proves the key signs as the app, then
seals it into its secret. The infrastructure app's key writes its own secret.
The release app's is written with your own token (`GITHUB_TOKEN`, or `gh`'s, as
an owner of `{{.GithubOrganization}}`) or as the infrastructure app, with a key
of that app (`--infrastructure-key <its file>`). Delete the file, then delete
the old key on the app's settings page. `bedrock org secrets check` says where
each key is, who reads it and when it was last sealed.

## Contributing

Changes to the organization are changes to `placement.json`, by pull request.
Read the plan the pull request's check posts before approving: it is what the
merge does.
